%% CASE Pixelforge game launch
Pixelforge Studios is developing a strategy game for PC and two game consoles. The launch date is fixed with the console makers and the publisher's marketing campaign. Three Scrum teams build the game in two-week sprints; an external vendor creates the 3D character models under a time-and-materials contract.

- Before launch, each console maker must certify the game. Certification takes **4 weeks**, and most games need **one resubmission**, which adds **2 weeks**.
- In the closed beta, the game crashes **3.2 times per 1,000 sessions**; the console makers require fewer than **1 per 1,000**. Crash reports show that **five crash types** cause most of the crashes.
- **30%** of the character models delivered by the vendor exceed the polygon budget defined in the art specification attached to the contract.
- After launch, the game will be run as a live service, earning revenue from optional expansions.
@@ PR8 | Predictive
How far ahead of the launch date should the teams plan to submit the game for certification?
A) At least six weeks, allowing for one resubmission
B) Four weeks, since certification takes four weeks
C) Two weeks, since a resubmission takes two weeks
D) One week, since the console makers usually certify games quickly
= A
? Planning must include the typical resubmission: 4 weeks of certification plus 2 weeks for one resubmission means submitting at least 6 weeks before launch.

@@ PR7 | Agile
What should the teams do first about the crash rate?
A) Fix the five most frequent crash types, tracking the crash rate on every build
B) Keep adding planned features and fix the crashes in a patch after the launch date
C) Ask the console makers to relax their crash requirement for this game
D) Add a manual test pass before each release to find crashes earlier
= A
? Most crashes come from a few causes, so fixing them gives the largest improvement. Tracking the rate on every build shows whether the target is being reached.

@@ PR5 | Hybrid
How should the project handle the character models that exceed the polygon budget?
A) Reject the non-conforming models under the contract specification and address the cause with the vendor
B) Accept the models and ask the studio's own artists to quietly fix them, so that the vendor is not upset
C) Terminate the contract immediately, since the vendor has failed to meet the agreed specification
D) Change the art specification to match the models, since the vendor's artists know best
= A
? The contract's specification is the basis for acceptance. Rejecting non-conforming work and addressing the cause with the vendor protects quality and the contract.

@@ PR3 | Agile
After launch, which measure best shows whether the game keeps delivering value to its players?
A) Player retention over time, such as the share still playing after 30 days
B) The number of copies sold in the first week after the launch, across all platforms
C) The number of features shipped by the live team each month
D) The number of developers working on the live team
= A
? For a live service, continued engagement shows lasting value. First-week sales and output measures do not show whether players stay.
%% END CASE

